# Qx visual design guide: visual check

```elixir
# The local Qx checkout, one directory above this notebook.
qx_path = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)

Mix.install([
  {:qx, path: qx_path},
  {:kino, "~> 0.12"},
  {:vega_lite, "~> 0.1.11"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1.11"}
])

pi = :math.pi()
```

## How to use this notebook

Run the cells in order. Each cell has an `# Expected:` comment that says what a correct picture looks like; compare the output with it.

The setup cell installs Qx from the local checkout (`notebooks/..`), so it shows the code on disk, not a Hex release. After changing Qx, use **Reconnect and setup** on the setup cell to recompile it.

The colours to look for:

| Family      | Stroke  | Gates                                    |
| ----------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Pauli       | blue    | X, Y, Z, the ⊕ of CNOT and Toffoli, CY |
| Hadamard    | orange  | H                                        |
| Phase       | green   | S, S†, T, T†, P(λ), controlled-P    |
| Rotation    | magenta | Rx, Ry, Rz, U, the controlled rotations  |
| Exchange    | purple  | SWAP, iSWAP, CSWAP                       |
| Non-unitary | grey    | measurement, condition, barrier          |

## 1. Circuit diagrams: single-qubit gates

```elixir
# Expected: one wire labelled q0 (italic q, upright subscript). Eight
# 32 x 32 boxes with a pale fill and a coloured outline:
# H orange; X, Y, Z blue; S, S†, T, T† green.
# Letters are italic serif; the dagger is a small superscript.
Qx.create_circuit(1)
|> Qx.h(0)
|> Qx.x(0)
|> Qx.y(0)
|> Qx.z(0)
|> Qx.s(0)
|> Qx.sdg(0)
|> Qx.t(0)
|> Qx.tdg(0)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Single-qubit gates")
```

```elixir
# Expected: boxes wider than the letter gates. Rx(π/2), Ry(π/4), Rz(−π/3)
# and U(π/2, 0, π) are magenta; P(π/8) is green. The axis letter is an
# italic subscript, angles are upright fractions of π, and the minus
# sign is a true minus (−), not a hyphen. No label touches its box.
Qx.create_circuit(1)
|> Qx.rx(0, pi / 2)
|> Qx.ry(0, pi / 4)
|> Qx.rz(0, -pi / 3)
|> Qx.phase(0, pi / 8)
|> Qx.u(0, pi / 2, 0, pi)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Rotations and phase")
```

```elixir
# Expected: an angle that is not a fraction of π is shown with two
# decimals: Rx(0.30), Ry(−1.23). An angle that rounds to zero is "0",
# never "−0.00": Rz(0). The boxes widen to fit.
Qx.create_circuit(1)
|> Qx.rx(0, 0.3)
|> Qx.ry(0, -1.23)
|> Qx.rz(0, -0.002)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Decimal angles")
```

## 2. Circuit diagrams: multi-qubit gates

```elixir
# Expected, left to right:
# CNOT: ink dot on q0, blue ⊕ on q1, ink line between.
# CZ: two ink dots, no colour.
# CY: ink dot joined to a blue Y box.
# SWAP: two purple crosses joined by an ink line.
# iSWAP on adjacent wires: ONE purple box spanning both wires, labelled
# iSWAP (italic i, upright SWAP).
Qx.create_circuit(2)
|> Qx.cx(0, 1)
|> Qx.cz(0, 1)
|> Qx.cy(0, 1)
|> Qx.swap(0, 1)
|> Qx.iswap(0, 1)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Two-qubit gates")
```

```elixir
# Expected: four controlled gates, each an ink dot joined to a box:
# P(π/2) green; Rx(π/2), Ry(π/4), Rz(π) magenta. The line stops at the
# edge of the box. These four and CY raised an error in 0.12.
Qx.create_circuit(2)
|> Qx.cp(0, 1, pi / 2)
|> Qx.crx(0, 1, pi / 2)
|> Qx.cry(0, 1, pi / 4)
|> Qx.crz(0, 1, pi)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Controlled gates with an angle")
```

```elixir
# Expected: Toffoli (two ink dots, blue ⊕ on q2), then Fredkin (ink dot
# on q0, purple crosses on q1 and q2), then an iSWAP on q0 and q2: two
# purple iSWAP boxes joined by an ink line that passes over q1.
Qx.create_circuit(3)
|> Qx.ccx(0, 1, 2)
|> Qx.cswap(0, 1, 2)
|> Qx.iswap(0, 2)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Three-qubit gates")
```

```elixir
# Expected: the control is on the LOWER wire and the target on the upper
# one in both gates: ⊕ on q0 with the dot on q1, then ⊕ on q0 with dots
# on q1 and q2.
Qx.create_circuit(3)
|> Qx.cx(1, 0)
|> Qx.ccx(2, 1, 0)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Controls below the target")
```

## 3. Circuit diagrams: measurement, conditions, barriers

```elixir
# Expected: quantum teleportation.
# - Three wires q0..q2, and under them a classical register: a double
#   grey line labelled c, with a slash and the number 2.
# - A dashed grey barrier after the Bell pair, in a column of its own.
# - Two meters (grey box, ink arc and needle). From each a double line
#   drops to an arrowhead on the register, with the bit index beside it.
# - X (blue) and Z (blue) on q2, each joined from below to a dot on the
#   register and labelled c1 = 1 and c0 = 1.
# - No line crosses a gate it does not belong to.
Qx.create_circuit(3, 2)
|> Qx.h(1)
|> Qx.cx(1, 2)
|> Qx.barrier([0, 1, 2])
|> Qx.cx(0, 1)
|> Qx.h(0)
|> Qx.measure(0, 0)
|> Qx.measure(1, 1)
|> Qx.c_if(1, 1, &Qx.x(&1, 2))
|> Qx.c_if(0, 1, &Qx.z(&1, 2))
|> Qx.draw_circuit("Quantum teleportation")
```

```elixir
# Expected: the conditional X on q1 has a column to itself: its line to
# the register does NOT run through the H on q2, which is drawn in the
# next column.
Qx.create_circuit(3, 1)
|> Qx.measure(0, 0)
|> Qx.c_if(0, 1, &Qx.x(&1, 1))
|> Qx.h(2)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("A condition line and a bystander")
```

```elixir
# Expected: the barrier covers only q0 and q1 (it stops short of q2),
# and the X added after it is drawn to its right.
Qx.create_circuit(3)
|> Qx.h(0)
|> Qx.barrier([0, 1])
|> Qx.x(0)
|> Qx.h(2)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("A partial barrier")
```

```elixir
# Expected: draws without an error (0.12 raised Qx.QubitIndexError):
# one qubit measured into classical bit 1 of a 2-bit register.
Qx.create_circuit(1, 2)
|> Qx.measure(0, 1)
|> Qx.draw_circuit("More classical bits than qubits")
```

## 4. Circuit diagrams: whole circuits and options

```elixir
# Expected: the three-qubit QFT. Columns line up: every gate in a column
# shares the width of the widest box in it. The P(π/2) and P(π/4) boxes
# are green and the same width. Ends with a barrier and a SWAP.
qft3 =
  Qx.create_circuit(3)
  |> Qx.h(0)
  |> Qx.cp(1, 0, pi / 2)
  |> Qx.cp(2, 0, pi / 4)
  |> Qx.h(1)
  |> Qx.cp(2, 1, pi / 2)
  |> Qx.h(2)
  |> Qx.barrier([0, 1, 2])
  |> Qx.swap(0, 2)

Qx.draw_circuit(qft3, "Three-qubit quantum Fourier transform")
```

```elixir
# Expected: the same diagram with no colour at all: every box is an ink
# outline on white, and the geometry is identical to the cell above.
Qx.draw_circuit(qft3, title: "QFT, mono style", style: :mono)
```

```elixir
# Expected: no title, and the first wire sits nearer the top of the
# image. The second picture (title: "") is identical to the first.
Kino.Layout.grid([Qx.draw_circuit(qft3), Qx.draw_circuit(qft3, title: "")], columns: 1)
```

```elixir
# Expected: the title reads  a < b & "c"  exactly, and the image is at
# least as wide as the title.
Qx.create_circuit(1)
|> Qx.h(0)
|> Qx.draw_circuit(~s(A long title with a < b & "c" in it, wider than the circuit))
```

```elixir
# Expected: a circuit that is the last value of a cell renders as its
# diagram (no draw call), in colour and with no title.
Qx.create_circuit(2) |> Qx.h(0) |> Qx.cx(0, 1)
```

## 5. Bloch sphere

```elixir
bloch = fn circuit, title -> circuit |> Qx.get_state() |> Qx.draw_bloch(title: title) end

# Expected: six spheres. Each has a softly shaded body with an ink
# outline, three great circles (solid on the near side, dashed and pale
# on the far side), grey axes labelled x, y, z in italics (x toward you
# and to the left, y to the right, z up), and six ket labels.
# The orange state vector is the only coloured mark.
#   |0⟩  straight up              θ = 0.00π  φ = 0.00π
#   |1⟩  straight down            θ = 1.00π  φ = 0.00π
#   |+⟩  along x, solid head      θ = 0.50π  φ = 0.00π
#   |−⟩  along −x, HOLLOW head    θ = 0.50π  φ = 1.00π
#   |i⟩  along y, solid head      θ = 0.50π  φ = 0.50π
#   |−i⟩ along −y, HOLLOW head    θ = 0.50π  φ = 1.50π  (never −0.50π)
Kino.Layout.grid(
  [
    bloch.(Qx.create_circuit(1), "|0⟩"),
    bloch.(Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.x(0), "|1⟩"),
    bloch.(Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.h(0), "|+⟩"),
    bloch.(Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.x(0) |> Qx.h(0), "|−⟩"),
    bloch.(Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.h(0) |> Qx.s(0), "|i⟩"),
    bloch.(Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.h(0) |> Qx.sdg(0), "|−i⟩")
  ],
  columns: 3
)
```

```elixir
# Expected: a general state, θ = 0.65π  φ = 0.45π. The vector points
# below the equator. A dashed orange line drops from its tip to the
# tinted equatorial plane, a faint line joins that foot to the centre,
# and two grey arcs are labelled θ (from the z axis) and φ (in the
# plane, from the x axis).
Qx.create_circuit(1)
|> Qx.ry(0, 0.65 * pi)
|> Qx.rz(0, 0.45 * pi)
|> Qx.get_state()
|> Qx.draw_bloch(title: "A general state")
```

```elixir
# Expected: the camera looks from 30° round from x and 18° above the
# equator. Left: the vector points straight at you and is drawn as a
# SOLID orange dot at the centre. Right: it points straight away and is
# a HOLLOW orange ring. Neither has an ink point inside it.
toward = Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.ry(0, 0.4 * pi) |> Qx.rz(0, pi / 6)
away = Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.ry(0, 0.6 * pi) |> Qx.rz(0, 7 * pi / 6)

Kino.Layout.grid([bloch.(toward, "Toward the reader"), bloch.(away, "Away from the reader")],
  columns: 2
)
```

```elixir
# Expected: the same state at 240, 400 and 640 pixels. Everything
# scales, but in the small one the text and lines are no thinner than
# 80% of the 400 size, so it stays legible. The last has no title and
# the sphere is centred.
state = Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.ry(0, 0.65 * pi) |> Qx.rz(0, 0.45 * pi) |> Qx.get_state()

Kino.Layout.grid(
  [
    Qx.draw_bloch(state, size: 240, title: "240"),
    Qx.draw_bloch(state, title: "400"),
    Qx.draw_bloch(state, size: 640, title: nil)
  ],
  columns: 1
)
```

## 6. Charts

```elixir
# Expected: a Bell state. Two blue bars at |00⟩ and |11⟩, each 0.50,
# with the value written above every bar (4 bars, so labels are shown).
# The y axis runs from 0 to 1 exactly, titled Probability; the x axis is
# titled "Basis state" with level ket labels in a monospace face. No
# frame round the plot, rounded bar tops, title at the top left.
bell = Qx.create_circuit(2, 2) |> Qx.h(0) |> Qx.cx(0, 1) |> Qx.measure(0, 0) |> Qx.measure(1, 1)
bell_result = Qx.run(bell, shots: 1000)

Qx.draw(bell_result, title: "Bell state probabilities")
```

```elixir
# Expected: the same result as counts: bars at |00⟩ and |11⟩ near 500
# each, the count written above each bar as a whole number, y axis
# titled Counts and starting at zero. Same blue, same style.
Qx.draw_counts(bell_result, title: "Bell state counts")
```

```elixir
# Expected: a uniform distribution over 8 states: eight equal bars with
# 0.12 above each (0.125 to two decimals; 8 bars is the limit for
# values). Every bar is under 0.2, so the axis fits the data: it ends
# just above 0.12 and the bars fill the chart.
Qx.create_circuit(3)
|> Qx.h_all()
|> Qx.get_probabilities()
|> Qx.draw_histogram(title: "Uniform over 8 states")
```

```elixir
# Expected: 32 equal bars that fill the chart: every bar is 0.031, so
# the axis fits the data (0 to 0.035) and is not 0 to 1. Past 16
# bars the labels become bare bit strings (00000, 00001, ...) set
# vertically, and past 8 bars there are no values above the bars.
Qx.create_circuit(5) |> Qx.h_all() |> Qx.run() |> Qx.draw(title: "32 basis states")
```

```elixir
# Expected: 128 equal bars that fill the chart, on an axis from 0 to
# about 0.008. Past 64 bars only every fourth label is shown.
Qx.create_circuit(7) |> Qx.h_all() |> Qx.run() |> Qx.draw(title: "128 basis states", width: 700)
```

```elixir
# Expected: a peaked distribution over 32 states: one tall bar at 00000
# (about 0.82) among very short ones. A bar reaches 0.2, so the axis is
# 0 to 1 again.
Qx.create_circuit(5)
|> Qx.ry(0, 0.4)
|> Qx.ry(1, 0.4)
|> Qx.ry(2, 0.4)
|> Qx.ry(3, 0.4)
|> Qx.ry(4, 0.4)
|> Qx.run()
|> Qx.draw(title: "Peaked over 32 states")
```

```elixir
# Expected: bars in bit-string order from 000000 upward, even though
# there are more than 32 distinct outcomes.
many = Qx.create_circuit(6, 6) |> Qx.h_all() |> Qx.measure_all()
Qx.run(many, shots: 4000) |> Qx.draw_counts(title: "64 outcomes, in order", width: 700)
```

```elixir
# Expected: an empty chart in the same style. The first is titled
# "No Measurements" (the default), the second "Run 7".
no_counts = Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.h(0) |> Qx.run()

Kino.Layout.grid([Qx.draw_counts(no_counts), Qx.draw_counts(no_counts, title: "Run 7")],
  columns: 2
)
```

## 7. Errors

Each row should show a typed `Qx.*Error`, not a `FunctionClauseError`, `ArithmeticError`, `MatchError` or `Enum.EmptyError`.

```elixir
circuit = Qx.create_circuit(2) |> Qx.h(0)
state = Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.get_state()
result = Qx.create_circuit(1) |> Qx.h(0) |> Qx.run()

cases = [
  {"unknown circuit option", fn -> Qx.draw_circuit(circuit, colour: :mono) end},
  {"invalid style", fn -> Qx.draw_circuit(circuit, style: :dark) end},
  {"empty barrier", fn -> circuit |> Qx.barrier([]) |> Qx.draw_circuit() end},
  {"angle too large to draw", fn -> circuit |> Qx.rx(0, 10 ** 400) |> Qx.draw_circuit() end},
  {"Bloch size too large", fn -> Qx.draw_bloch(state, size: 1.0e160) end},
  {"unknown Bloch option", fn -> Qx.draw_bloch(state, radius: 3) end},
  {"two-qubit state on the Bloch sphere",
   fn -> Qx.create_circuit(2) |> Qx.get_state() |> Qx.draw_bloch() end},
  {"unknown chart option", fn -> Qx.draw(result, colour: "red") end}
]

# Expected: every row names a Qx error:
# OptionError, OptionError, GateError, GateError, OptionError,
# OptionError, StateShapeError, OptionError.
rows =
  for {name, fun} <- cases do
    outcome =
      try do
        fun.()
        "no error raised"
      rescue
        error -> "#{inspect(error.__struct__)}: #{error |> Exception.message() |> String.slice(0, 90)}"
      end

    %{case: name, outcome: outcome}
  end

Kino.DataTable.new(rows, keys: [:case, :outcome])
```
